# Bitwise operations on integers. The generic interface: each public verb delegates to the native implementation for the build target, which lives in its own per-backend file (`native/<env>/bit`). All backends operate on 64-bit integers (the node and browser shims run through BigInt, exact within the 53-bit safe-integer range a host number lives under). `shift-right` is arithmetic (sign-preserving).

note stable

load @term/base/code/native/{platform}/bit
  find bit-and
  find bit-or
  find bit-exclusive-or
  find bit-not
  find bit-shift-left
  find bit-shift-right
  find bit-shift-right-unsigned
  find bit-multiply-32

task bitwise-and
  take left, like number
  take right, like number
  like number
  send back
    call bit-and
      read left
      read right

task bitwise-or
  take left, like number
  take right, like number
  like number
  send back
    call bit-or
      read left
      read right

task bitwise-exclusive-or
  take left, like number
  take right, like number
  like number
  send back
    call bit-exclusive-or
      read left
      read right

task bitwise-not
  take value, like number
  like number
  send back
    call bit-not
      read value

task shift-left
  take value, like number
  take count, like number
  like number
  send back
    call bit-shift-left
      read value
      read count

task shift-right
  take value, like number
  take count, like number
  like number
  send back
    call bit-shift-right
      read value
      read count

# a LOGICAL right shift: zeroes are shifted in, so the sign bit is not replicated. `shift-right` is arithmetic.
task unsigned-shift-right
  take value, like number
  take count, like number
  like number
  send back
    call bit-shift-right-unsigned
      read value
      read count

# A SIGNED 32-BIT MULTIPLY WITH WRAPAROUND, which is `Math.imul` on a JavaScript host and the only multiply the
# classic string hashes are defined in terms of. Ordinary multiplication is not a substitute: cyrb53 and every
# hash like it rely on the high bits being DISCARDED, so a 64-bit product gives a different number and a cache
# keyed on it stops agreeing with itself.
#
# Added 2026-09-02 for self-hosting: `hashText` is the compiler's content-addressed cache key, and it could not be
# written in Term at all without this. Found by trying to port it (self-hosting-0002).
task multiply-32
  take left, like number
  take right, like number
  like number
  send back
    call bit-multiply-32
      read left
      read right
